  Donald Knuth's \WEB\ system of literate programming spawned many other
  \WEB-like frameworks that worked, well, mostly like \WEB. But one of
  \WEB's ancestors did its tangling, then called synthesizing, differently.
  Pierre-Arnoul de~Marneffe's language of ``holons'' envisioned a system
  for writing structured programs that included sophisticated compilation
  techniques and a notion of scope
  for the code chunks.
  We glance at a few of these long-dormant ideas from \WEB's
  predecessor and note their reappearance in the literate programming
  tool Inweb.
